Accurate estimation of one-dimensional axial and lateral tissue displacements is critical for effective ultrasound elastography. Achieving high spatial and temporal resolution in both directions enables reliable estimation of strain tensors and eigenvalues, forming the basis for accurate tissue characterization. Historically, axial estimation accuracy has surpassed lateral accuracy; however, with the emergence of high-density linear array transducers offering superior lateral sampling, the gap between the two is narrowing.

This paper evaluates lateral tissue displacement estimation in ultrasound elastography using a 30 MHz high-frequency linear array in a phantom study targeting homogeneous regions. A custom-built mechanical stage moved the phantom laterally by 100 μm over a 1.5 mm range at 2 mm/min, data captured via a 1.5 MHz central frequency system with a 68 μs (approx. 10 samples) integration time. Processing involved computing the normalized cross-correlation between 150-μm (axial) segments before and after shifting, with results averaged across 10 realizations. For comparison, two approaches were utilized: (i) "conventional" overlapping 90% correlation with a 120-μm axial block, and (ii) a "non-overlapping" correlation with 50% correlation using the exact same 120-μm axial block.

